{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghpgULMVyQWXubkUg8cR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Dq ≈ 452 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 567 cm⁻¹** (with C = 4.5B ≈ 2552 cm⁻¹). The two bands fix Dq and B uniquely once the unphysical root is discarded. I solved this by hand and did not run the tools.\n\n**Model.** Tetrahedral d⁸ has the same term matrices as octahedral d², with Dq taken as the positive tetrahedral value. Energies are measured relative to ³F:\n\n- ³T₁(F) and ³T₁(P) mix through the matrix [[−6Dq, 6B], [6B, 15B + 6Dq]]. Its eigenvalues are E± = 7.5B ± √((7.5B + 6Dq)² + 36B²).\n- ³T₂ lies at 2Dq.\n- The ground state is the lower eigenvalue, ³T₁(F) = E₋.\n\n**Equations.** The ³T₁(2) state is the upper eigenvalue E₊, and C drops out of the spin-allowed ³T₁–³T₁ mixing.\n\n1. ν₃ = E₊ − E₋ = 2√((7.5B + 6Dq)² + 36B²) = 15 500. This gives √(…) = 7750.\n2. ν₁ = 2Dq − E₋ = 2Dq − 7.5B + 7750 = 4400. This gives Dq = 3.75B − 1675.\n\n**Solution.** Substituting Dq into the first equation gives 7.5B + 6Dq = 30B − 10 050. The condition becomes (30B − 10 050)² + 36B² = 7750², which expands to 936B² − 603 000B + 40 940 000 = 0.\n\n- The roots are B ≈ 77 and B ≈ 567 cm⁻¹. The first is unphysical, since it would imply a Dq below zero and a nephelauxetic ratio near 0.07.\n- Taking B = 567 cm⁻¹ gives Dq = 3.75(567) − 1675 ≈ 452 cm⁻¹, so Δt ≈ 4520 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Check.** With these values, 7.5B + 6Dq ≈ 6963, and √(6963² + 36·567²) ≈ 7749. That reproduces ν₃ = 15 500 cm⁻¹, and ν₁ = 4400 cm⁻¹ follows from the second equation.\n\n{\"Dq\": 452, \"B\": 567, \"C\": 2552, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Two bands (3T2 and 3T1(P)) with C/B fixed determine Dq and B for tetrahedral d8 via the 3T1 matrix.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8024,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8024,"ephemeral_